result:
Fixed. The cause was double-encoding of the abstract-namespace marker.

`std::os::linux::net::SocketAddrExt::from_abstract_name` takes the bare name and prepends the NUL itself. Both `UnixListener::bind` (`tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:83`) and `UnixStream::connect` (`tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:79`) detected the leading NUL to decide the address was abstract, then passed the *whole* string — NUL included — to `from_abstract_name`. So binding `"\0aaa"` actually registered the abstract name `"\0aaa"` (kernel-level bytes `\0\0aaa`), and `local_addr().as_abstract_name()` returned `b"\0aaa"` instead of `b"aaa"`. Bind and connect were wrong in matching ways, so connecting still worked — the name just wasn't the one requested, and it wouldn't interoperate with a peer that bound the same name correctly.

The change replaces the `starts_with` check with `strip_prefix`, so the marker NUL is consumed by the detection rather than passed along:

```rust
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verification: `abstract_socket_name` in `tokio/tests/uds_stream.rs` now passes, along with the full `uds_stream`, `uds_datagram`, and `uds_split` suites (21 tests, 0 failures). Note `cargo fmt` isn't installed in this environment, so the formatting check couldn't be run; the edit follows the surrounding style. `UnixSocket::bind`/`connect` in `tokio/src/net/unix/socket.rs` take a different route (`socket2::SockAddr::unix`) and were not touched.
